<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  Article 11 - Projectiles on an Inclined Plane, written as a PreTeXt <activity>.
  Notation follows the Section 1.3 webpage:
      v_0   initial speed          (book's  u)
      theta launch/elevation angle (book's  alpha)
      beta  inclination of plane
      g     acceleration of gravity
  The figure is a PreFigure <diagram> embedded directly inside <image>.
-->

<section xml:id="sec-projectile-incline">
  <title>Projectiles on an Inclined Plane</title>

  <activity xml:id="activity-projectile-incline">
    <title>Range up (and down) an inclined plane</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A particle is launched from a point <m>O</m> on an inclined plane and
        travels in the vertical plane that contains the line of greatest slope
        through <m>O</m>. The plane makes an angle <m>\beta</m> with the
        horizontal, and the particle leaves <m>O</m> with initial speed
        <m>v_0</m> at an elevation <m>\theta</m> to the horizontal. Taking the
        origin at <m>O</m> with horizontal and vertical axes and neglecting air
        resistance, the trajectory is the projectile parabola
        <md>
          y = x\tan\theta - \frac{g\,x^2}{2\,v_0^2\cos^2\theta}\text{.}
        </md>
        Let <m>P</m> be the point where the particle meets the plane again, and
        let <m>R = OP</m> be the range measured <em>along the plane</em>, so that
        <m>P = \bigl(R\cos\beta,\ R\sin\beta\bigr)</m>. Work through the tasks
        below to find the range on the plane, the maximum range for a given
        <m>v_0</m>, and the time of flight, and then to read off the
        corresponding results when the particle is fired <em>down</em> the plane.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-projectile-incline">
        <caption>
          A particle projected up the plane with speed <m>v_0</m> at elevation
          <m>\theta</m>, striking the incline (angle <m>\beta</m>) at <m>P</m>
          after travelling a range <m>R=OP</m> along the slope.
        </caption>
        <image width="85%" xml:id="image-projectile-incline">
          <!-- PreFigure source embedded inline -->
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-projectile-incline">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,280)" margins="5">
            <definition>f(x) = 1.2*x - 0.1*x^2</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.2, -1, 10.5, 5)">
              <axes at="pi-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
              <!-- the inclined plane (line of greatest slope) -->
              <line at="pi-plane" p1="(0,0)" p2="(9.6,3.84)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
              <!-- the parabolic trajectory -->
              <graph at="pi-path" domain="(0,8)" function="f" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
              <!-- range R along the plane from O to P -->
              <line at="pi-range" p1="(0,0)" p2="(8,3.2)" stroke="black" thickness="1"/>
              <!-- vertical projection R sin(beta) from foot M up to P -->
              <line at="pi-rise" p1="(8,0)" p2="(8,3.2)" stroke="gray" dash="4"/>
              <!-- initial velocity direction -->
              <line at="pi-v0" p1="(0,0)" p2="(1.6,1.92)" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              <!-- angle markers: beta from horizontal to incline, theta from horizontal to velocity -->
              <angle-marker at="pi-beta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(2,0.8))" radius="26" stroke="black"><m>\beta</m></angle-marker>
              <angle-marker at="pi-theta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(1.6,1.92))" radius="46" stroke="black" offset="(0,10)"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
              <point at="pi-O" p="(0,0)" size="3"/>
              <point at="pi-P" p="(8,3.2)" size="3"/>
              <!-- labels -->
              <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
              <label anchor="(8,3.2)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,10)"><m>P</m></label>
              <label anchor="(8,0)" alignment="s"><m>M</m></label>
              <label anchor="(1.6,1.92)" alignment="nw"><m>v_0</m></label>
              <label anchor="(4,1.6)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,6)"><m>R</m></label>
              <label anchor="(8,1.6)" alignment="e"><m>R\sin\beta</m></label>
              <label anchor="(4,0)" alignment="s"><m>R\cos\beta</m></label>
              <label anchor="(6,2.25)" alignment="s"><m>\text{inclined plane}</m></label>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A particle is launched from the point O at the foot of an inclined plane and follows a parabolic path, striking the plane again at the point P. The range O P measured along the plane is labelled R.">
              <annotation ref="pi-axes" text="Horizontal and vertical axes with origin at the launch point O."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-plane" text="The inclined plane, a straight line rising to the right from O at angle beta above the horizontal."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-path" text="The parabolic trajectory of the particle, drawn in blue from O up and over to the landing point P."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-v0" text="The initial velocity v naught at O, directed at elevation angle theta above the horizontal."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-range" text="The range R measured along the plane from O to P."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-rise" text="The vertical rise R sine beta from the foot M on the horizontal up to P."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-beta" text="The angle beta between the horizontal and the inclined plane."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-theta" text="The angle theta between the horizontal and the initial velocity."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-O" text="The launch point O at the origin."/>
              <annotation ref="pi-P" text="The point P where the particle strikes the plane again."/>
            </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-incline-onpath">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Since <m>P=\bigl(R\cos\beta,\ R\sin\beta\bigr)</m> lies on the
          trajectory, substitute these coordinates into the parabola and cancel a
          factor of <m>R</m> to show that
          <md>
            \sin\beta = \cos\beta\,\tan\theta
              - \frac{g\,R\,\cos^2\beta}{2\,v_0^2\cos^2\theta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Putting <m>x=R\cos\beta</m> and <m>y=R\sin\beta</m> into
          <m>y = x\tan\theta - \dfrac{g x^2}{2 v_0^2\cos^2\theta}</m> gives
          <md>
            R\sin\beta = R\cos\beta\,\tan\theta
              - \frac{g\,R^2\cos^2\beta}{2\,v_0^2\cos^2\theta}\text{.}
          </md>
          Dividing through by <m>R</m> (the particle is not at <m>O</m>) yields the
          stated relation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-incline-range">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rearrange the relation from the previous task to isolate <m>R</m>, and
          use <m>\sin\theta\cos\beta - \cos\theta\sin\beta = \sin(\theta-\beta)</m>
          to obtain the <term>range up the plane</term>
          <md>
            R = \frac{2\,v_0^2\,\sin(\theta-\beta)\cos\theta}{g\cos^2\beta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Collect the <m>R</m>-term:
          <md>
            \frac{g\,R\,\cos^2\beta}{2\,v_0^2\cos^2\theta}
              = \cos\beta\,\tan\theta - \sin\beta
              = \frac{\sin\theta\cos\beta - \cos\theta\sin\beta}{\cos\theta}
              = \frac{\sin(\theta-\beta)}{\cos\theta}\text{.}
          </md>
          Solving for <m>R</m>,
          <md>
            R = \frac{2\,v_0^2\cos^2\theta}{g\cos^2\beta}\cdot
                \frac{\sin(\theta-\beta)}{\cos\theta}
              = \frac{2\,v_0^2\,\sin(\theta-\beta)\cos\theta}{g\cos^2\beta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-incline-maxrange">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the identity
          <m>2\sin(\theta-\beta)\cos\theta = \sin(2\theta-\beta) - \sin\beta</m>,
          rewrite the range as
          <md>
            R = \frac{v_0^2}{g\cos^2\beta}
                \bigl[\sin(2\theta-\beta) - \sin\beta\bigr]\text{.}
          </md>
          For a fixed launch speed <m>v_0</m>, deduce the elevation <m>\theta</m>
          that maximizes <m>R</m>, and show that the maximum range up the plane is
          <md>
            R_{\max} = \frac{v_0^2}{g\,(1+\sin\beta)}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          With <m>v_0</m>, <m>g</m>, and <m>\beta</m> held constant, only
          <m>\sin(2\theta-\beta)</m> varies, and it is largest when it equals
          <m>1</m>. Afterwards use <m>\cos^2\beta = (1-\sin\beta)(1+\sin\beta)</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>R</m> is greatest when <m>\sin(2\theta-\beta)=1</m>, i.e. when
          <m>2\theta-\beta = \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, giving the optimal elevation
          <md>
            \theta = \frac{\pi}{4} + \frac{\beta}{2}\text{.}
          </md>
          At this value,
          <md>
            R_{\max} = \frac{v_0^2}{g\cos^2\beta}\,(1-\sin\beta)
              = \frac{v_0^2\,(1-\sin\beta)}{g\,(1-\sin\beta)(1+\sin\beta)}
              = \frac{v_0^2}{g\,(1+\sin\beta)}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-incline-tof">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The horizontal distance <m>OM</m> to the foot of <m>P</m> is covered at
          the constant horizontal speed <m>v_0\cos\theta</m>. Using
          <m>OM = R\cos\beta</m>, show that the <term>time of flight</term> is
          <md>
            T = \frac{2\,v_0\,\sin(\theta-\beta)}{g\cos\beta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From <m>R\cos\beta = v_0\cos\theta\cdot T</m>,
          <md>
            T = \frac{R\cos\beta}{v_0\cos\theta}
              = \frac{\cos\beta}{v_0\cos\theta}\cdot
                \frac{2\,v_0^2\,\sin(\theta-\beta)\cos\theta}{g\cos^2\beta}
              = \frac{2\,v_0\,\sin(\theta-\beta)}{g\cos\beta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-incline-down">
      <statement>
        <p>
          When the particle is projected <em>down</em> the plane, the geometry is
          the same with <m>\beta</m> replaced by <m>-\beta</m>. Write down the
          range, maximum range, and time of flight down the inclined plane.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <answer>
        <p>
          Replacing <m>\beta \to -\beta</m>:
          <md>
            R_{\text{down}} =
              \frac{2\,v_0^2\,\sin(\theta+\beta)\cos\theta}{g\cos^2\beta}\text{,}
            \qquad
            R_{\max,\text{down}} = \frac{v_0^2}{g\,(1-\sin\beta)}\text{,}
          </md>
          <md>
            T_{\text{down}} = \frac{2\,v_0\,\sin(\theta+\beta)}{g\cos\beta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </answer>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        The maximum range up the plane occurs when
        <m>2\theta - \beta = \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, which rearranges to
        <m>\theta - \beta = \dfrac{\pi}{2} - \theta</m>. The left side is the angle
        the launch direction makes <em>above the incline</em>, and the right side
        is the angle it makes <em>below the vertical</em>. Since they are equal,
        the direction of projection for maximum range up an inclined plane
        bisects the angle between the upward vertical through <m>O</m> and the
        line of greatest slope.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </activity>

  <figure xml:id="fig-velocity-at-P">
    <caption>
      A particle projected from <m>O</m> with speed <m>v_0</m> at elevation
      <m>\theta</m>. At the point <m>P</m> reached after time <m>t</m>, the
      velocity <m>v</m> is tangent to the path and makes an angle <m>\phi</m>
      with the horizontal; <m>h</m> is the depth of <m>P</m> below the directrix.
    </caption>
    <image width="85%" xml:id="image-velocity-at-P">
      <!-- PreFigure source embedded inline -->
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-velocity-at-P">
      <diagram dimensions="(420,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 0.2*x*(8 - x)</definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-1.2, -1, 9.6, 5.4)">
          <axes at="vp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
          <!-- directrix (for the corollary) -->
          <line at="vp-directrix" p1="(-0.5,4.45)" p2="(8.8,4.45)" stroke="gray" dash="4"/>
          <label anchor="(3.3,4.45)" alignment="n"><m>\text{directrix}</m></label>
          <!-- parabolic trajectory -->
          <graph at="vp-path" domain="(0,8)" function="f" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
          <!-- vertical fall from the directrix down to P -->
          <line at="vp-h" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(6,4.45)" stroke="gray" dash="4"/>
          <label anchor="(6,3.42)" alignment="w"><m>h</m></label>
          <!-- initial velocity at O -->
          <line at="vp-v0" p1="(0,0)" p2="(1.06,1.70)" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
          <angle-marker at="vp-theta" points="((1,0),(0,0),(1.06,1.70))" radius="30" stroke="black"><m>\theta</m></angle-marker>
          <label anchor="(1.06,1.70)" alignment="nw"><m>v_0</m></label>
          <!-- velocity components triangle at P -->
          <line at="vp-vcos" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(7.64,2.4)" stroke="gray" dash="3"/>
          <line at="vp-vsin" p1="(7.64,2.4)" p2="(7.64,1.09)" stroke="gray" dash="3"/>
          <label anchor="(6.82,2.4)" alignment="n"><m>v\cos\phi</m></label>
          <label anchor="(7.64,1.745)" alignment="e"><m>v\sin\phi</m></label>
          <!-- velocity vector at P (tangent to path) -->
          <line at="vp-v" p1="(6,2.4)" p2="(7.64,1.09)" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
          <angle-marker at="vp-phi" points="((7.64,1.09),(6,2.4),(7.64,2.4))" radius="26" stroke="black"><m>\phi</m></angle-marker>
          <label anchor="(6.82,1.745)" alignment="sw" offset="(-2,-2)"><m>v</m></label>
          <!-- key points -->
          <point at="vp-O" p="(0,0)" size="3"/>
          <point at="vp-P" p="(6,2.4)" size="3"/>
          <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
          <label anchor="(6,2.4)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,6)"><m>P</m></label>
        </coordinates>

        <annotations>
        <annotation ref="figure" text="A particle is launched from O and follows a parabolic path. At the point P reached after time t, the velocity v is tangent to the path and makes an angle phi with the horizontal. A horizontal directrix lies above the path, and h is the depth of P below it.">
          <annotation ref="vp-axes" text="Horizontal and vertical axes with origin at the launch point O."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-path" text="The parabolic trajectory of the particle, drawn in blue."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-directrix" text="The directrix, a horizontal dashed line above the trajectory."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-h" text="The vertical depth h of the point P below the directrix."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-v0" text="The initial velocity v naught at O, directed at elevation angle theta above the horizontal."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-theta" text="The angle theta between the horizontal and the initial velocity at O."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-v" text="The velocity v at P, tangent to the path, pointing down and to the right."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-phi" text="The angle phi between the horizontal and the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-vcos" text="The horizontal component v cosine phi of the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-vsin" text="The vertical component v sine phi of the velocity at P."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-O" text="The launch point O at the origin."/>
          <annotation ref="vp-P" text="The point P on the path reached after time t."/>
        </annotation>
        </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-projectile-velocity">
    <title>Velocity and direction after a given time</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exercise-velocity-direction">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A particle is projected from <m>O</m> with speed <m>v_0</m> at an
          elevation <m>\theta</m> to the horizontal, as in
          <xref ref="fig-velocity-at-P"/>. At time <m>t</m> it is at the point
          <m>P</m>, moving with speed <m>v</m> in a direction making an angle
          <m>\phi</m> with the horizontal. Show that
          <md>
            v = \sqrt{\,v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2\,}
          </md>
          and
          <md>
            \tan\phi = \frac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\text{.}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Take the origin at <m>O</m> with horizontal and vertical axes. The
          coordinates of <m>P</m> at time <m>t</m> are
          <md>
            x = v_0\cos\theta\;t\text{,} \qquad
            y = v_0\sin\theta\;t - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2\text{.}
          </md>
          The horizontal and vertical components of the velocity are the time
          derivatives of these, so
          <md>
            v\cos\phi = \frac{dx}{dt} = v_0\cos\theta\text{,} \qquad
            v\sin\phi = \frac{dy}{dt} = v_0\sin\theta - g\,t\text{.}
          </md>
          Squaring and adding eliminates <m>\phi</m>:
          <md>
            v^2 = v_0^2\cos^2\theta + \bigl(v_0\sin\theta - g\,t\bigr)^2
                = v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2\text{,}
          </md>
          which gives the speed at time <m>t</m>. Dividing the vertical component
          by the horizontal component gives the direction,
          <md>
            \tan\phi = \frac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\text{,}
          </md>
          that is, <m>\phi = \arctan\!\left(\dfrac{v_0\sin\theta - g\,t}{v_0\cos\theta}\right)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exercise-directrix-freefall">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>directrix</term> of the parabolic path is the horizontal line
          at height <m>\dfrac{v_0^2}{2g}</m> above <m>O</m>. Using the previous
          exercise, show that the speed of the particle at <m>P</m> is equal to the
          speed it would acquire by falling freely from the level of the directrix
          down to <m>P</m>. That is, if <m>h</m> denotes the depth of <m>P</m>
          below the directrix (see <xref ref="fig-velocity-at-P"/>), then
          <m>v^2 = 2\,g\,h</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From the previous exercise,
          <md>
            v^2 = v_0^2 - 2\,v_0\,g\,t\sin\theta + g^2 t^2
                = v_0^2 - 2g\!\left(v_0\,t\sin\theta - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2\right)
                = v_0^2 - 2 g y\text{,}
          </md>
          since <m>y = v_0\,t\sin\theta - \tfrac{1}{2}g\,t^2</m> is the height of
          <m>P</m>. Writing this as
          <md>
            v^2 = 2g\!\left(\frac{v_0^2}{2g} - y\right)
          </md>
          and recognizing <m>\dfrac{v_0^2}{2g}</m> as the height of the directrix
          above <m>O</m>, the quantity <m>h = \dfrac{v_0^2}{2g} - y</m> is exactly
          the depth of <m>P</m> below the directrix. Hence <m>v^2 = 2\,g\,h</m>,
          the square of the speed gained by falling freely through the height
          <m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
